  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts how lithium metal cycling behaves at ultra-low temperature depending on solvent–ion interaction strength. In both schematics, a negatively polarized electrode sits on the left and a solvated positive ion approaches from the electrolyte. The blue shapes represent surrounding solvent molecules, while gray spheres indicate deposited lithium on the electrode surface.\n\nThe main message is that weak solvent–ion interactions favor cleaner lithium deposition. In the left panel, the solvated ion appears to release lithium more directly at the electrode, producing a relatively compact, uniform layer of deposited particles.\n\nIn the right panel, a thermometer and snowflake symbols indicate much colder conditions. Here, strong solvent–ion interactions keep the ion more tightly coordinated by solvent molecules. Curved arrows suggest slower or more complicated interfacial motion and desolvation before lithium can deposit. The resulting gray deposits near the electrode look more irregular and clustered, implying less controlled plating.\n\nOverall, the visual argues that under ultra-low-temperature operation, the strength of solvent binding to lithium is a key factor: weaker interactions make ion transfer and deposition easier, whereas stronger interactions hinder the process and promote uneven lithium growth.",
